    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Basque kingdom of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pamplona">Pamplona</a>, which emerged around 824 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and the Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III the Great</a> (1004–1035) it dominated Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a>, and his sons founded the royal lines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Aragon.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> conquered its southern part in 1512, but Navarre kept its own laws until 1841, while its northern kings became kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 958 the elderly Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#toda-of-pamplona">Toda of Pamplona</a> travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cordoba">Córdoba</a> with her grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sancho-i-of-leon">Sancho the Fat of León</a>, who was treated there by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a>’s physician for his obesity.</p></aside>
    </section>

