    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlemagne.jpg" alt="Charlemagne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne">Charlemagne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlemagne_denier_Mayence_812_814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charlemagne. Autorité émettrice de monnaie indéterminé. Atelier monétaire. Émetteur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pamplona">Pamplona</a>, later called Navarre, arose among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basques">Basques</a> of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, a people who had long resisted both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#visigoths">Visigoths</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. After the Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a>, Pamplona was disputed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cordoba">Córdoba</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>, and the Basques ambushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>’s rearguard at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-roncevaux-pass">Roncevaux</a> in 778. Around 824 a second Frankish expedition was destroyed in the same passes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inigo-arista">Íñigo Arista</a> emerged as ruler of Pamplona. His power rested on an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a>, a Muslim family of Hispano-Gothic descent ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tudela">Tudela</a> and the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebro-river">Ebro</a>, whose leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#musa-ibn-musa">Musa ibn Musa</a> was Íñigo’s half-brother through their mother. The early rulers sometimes paid tribute to Córdoba, and their lands were small, centred on Pamplona and the valleys around it. In 905 a rival family took power, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sancho-garces-i-of-pamplona">Sancho Garcés I</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jimenez-dynasty">Jimena dynasty</a>, broke with the Banu Qasi and expanded southward. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ordono-ii-of-leon">Ordoño II of León</a> he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#najera">Nájera</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#la-rioja-spain">La Rioja</a> in the 920s, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> of Córdoba defeated the Christians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-valdejunquera">Valdejunquera</a> in 920 and sacked Pamplona in 924. Through marriage the Jimena kings also gained the neighbouring county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, laying the foundations for later expansion across the Pyrenees.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#tudela"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tudela.jpg" alt="Tudela" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#tudela">Tudela</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tudela_panor%C3%A1mica_(7796714018)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Albert Torelló, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Navarre was a land of contrasts. Its northern valleys were mountain country of herders who moved flocks between Pyrenean summer pastures and the lowlands, while the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ribera-navarra">Ribera</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebro-river">Ebro</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tudela">Tudela</a>, produced grain, wine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#olive-oil">olive oil</a> with irrigation systems inherited from the Muslim period. Most of the population spoke Basque, which a twelfth-century document calls the <em>lingua navarrorum</em>, the language of the Navarrese, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> and a local Romance were used in writing, and Occitan by communities of settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>. These <em>francos</em> came with the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>, which crossed the kingdom from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-roncevaux-pass">Roncesvalles</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pamplona">Pamplona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#puente-la-reina">Puente la Reina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#estella-lizarra">Estella</a>; kings granted them charters and privileges, and towns such as Estella, founded around 1090, grew on the trade of the pilgrim road. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I the Battler</a> took Tudela in 1119, the kingdom included substantial Muslim and Jewish communities. Tudela’s Jewish community produced the twelfth-century traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#benjamin-of-tudela">Benjamin of Tudela</a>, whose account describes Jewish communities from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#monastery-of-leyre">Leire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#monastery-of-irache">Irache</a> were major landholders, and royal revenue came from crown estates, tolls on the Pyrenean routes and taxes granted by the cortes. Navarre always remained a small, largely rural realm.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa.jpg" alt="Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_las_Navas_de_Tolosa,_por_Francisco_van_Halen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francisco de Paula Van Halen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Navarre’s armies were drawn from the nobility, the free peasants of the mountain valleys and town militias, and in the early centuries they fought mostly in raids and frontier campaigns against the Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley, often suffering heavy reprisals from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cordoba">Córdoba</a>. The kingdom’s greatest expansion came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III the Great</a> (1004–1035), who through war, marriage and diplomacy extended his authority over Aragon, Sobrarbe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ribagorza">Ribagorza</a> and, from 1029, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and in 1034 occupied León. After his death the dynasties of his sons fought each other: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#garcia-sanchez-iii-of-pamplona">García Sánchez III of Pamplona</a> was killed in 1054 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-atapuerca">Battle of Atapuerca</a> by the army of his brother Ferdinand I of Castile. Partition followed the murder of Sancho IV at Peñalén in 1076, and in 1200 Navarre lost Álava and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gipuzkoa">Gipuzkoa</a>, and with them its coast, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII of Castile</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sancho-vii-of-navarre">Sancho VII the Strong</a> led a Navarrese contingent at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212, where the Christian kings broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> army; later tradition credited him with breaking through the chains around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a>’s camp. The kingdom’s last wars were defensive. In 1512 a Castilian army under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pamplona">Pamplona</a>, and in 1521 a Franco-Navarrese army briefly retook the city, where the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ignatius-of-loyola">Ignatius of Loyola</a> was wounded defending the citadel, before its defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-noain">Noáin</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#fuero-general-de-navarra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fuero-general-de-navarra.jpg" alt="Fuero General de Navarra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#fuero-general-de-navarra">Fuero General de Navarra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NafarForuak.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">No machine-readable author provided. Mgoni4 assumed (based on copyright claims)., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early rulers styled themselves kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pamplona">Pamplona</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sancho-vi-of-navarre">Sancho VI the Wise</a> (1150–1194) was the first to use the title king of Navarre consistently. Kings ruled with the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishop</a> of Pamplona, the great abbeys and a high nobility of <em>ricoshombres</em>, but the kingdom’s distinctive feature was the strength of its customary law, the <em>fueros</em>. Sancho VI granted fueros to towns such as Vitoria, which he founded in 1181, and in the thirteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fuero-general-de-navarra">Fuero General de Navarra</a> was compiled, defining the mutual obligations of king and subjects; it required each new king to swear to uphold the kingdom’s laws before receiving his subjects’ oath of loyalty. The cortes, bringing together clergy, nobility and towns, emerged as a regular assembly in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries and voted taxation. After 1234 the crown passed to the counts of Champagne, then in 1274 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#joan-i-of-navarre">Joan I</a>, whose marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV</a> joined Navarre to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> until 1328, and later to the houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#evreux">Évreux</a>, Foix and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-albret">Albret</a>. Absent kings governed through governors, a royal council and a high court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-iii-of-navarre">Charles III the Noble</a> (1387–1425) built the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#olite">Olite</a> and in 1423 united the three rival boroughs of Pamplona by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#privilege-of-the-union">Privilege of the Union</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#pamplona"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pamplona.jpg" alt="Pamplona" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#pamplona">Pamplona</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monumento_a_los_Fueros_(Pamplona).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luis Filpo Cabana, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For a small kingdom Navarre had a large influence on the shape of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III the Great</a>’s division of his realms among his sons created separate kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> under his descendants, so that the ruling houses of medieval Castile, León and Aragon all traced their royal line to the Jimena kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pamplona">Pamplona</a>. By favouring the pilgrimage route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#santiago">Santiago</a> and its Frankish settlers, Navarre helped tie northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> to the Romanesque culture and church reform of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>; the route, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#camino-frances">Camino Francés</a>, remains one of the most travelled pilgrim roads in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. Its rulers were closely linked to French courts: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#theobald-i-of-navarre">Theobald I of Champagne</a> was among the best known trouvères of his day, and through Jeanne d’<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-albret">Albret</a> and her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-iv-of-france">Henry III of Navarre</a>, who became Henry IV of France in 1589, Navarre gave France its Bourbon dynasty. French kings styled themselves kings of France and Navarre for most of the period until 1830. The kingdom’s most durable legacy, however, is constitutional. Its fueros and institutions survived the conquest of 1512 and the end of the kingdom in 1841, and the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#foral-community-of-navarre">Foral Community of Navarre</a>, with its own fiscal regime agreed with the Spanish state, descends directly from them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-wars.jpg" alt="Italian Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars">Italian Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italian_War_Battle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Waylon1104, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom was weakened in the fifteenth century by a long civil war. When Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#blanche-i-of-navarre">Blanche I</a> died in 1441, her husband John, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-ii-of-aragon">John II of Aragon</a>, kept the crown instead of yielding it to their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-prince-of-viana">Charles, Prince of Viana</a>, and the nobility split into the Agramontese faction supporting John and the Beaumontese supporting Charles. The conflict outlived Charles’s death in 1461 and left Navarre dependent on its powerful neighbours. In 1512, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand the Catholic</a> accused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-albret">Albret</a> monarchs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-iii-duke-of-cleves">John III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#catherine-of-navarre">Catherine of Foix</a>, of siding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> and sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pamplona">Pamplona</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#upper-navarre">Upper Navarre</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, was incorporated into the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> by the cortes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgos">Burgos</a> in 1515, keeping its own laws, cortes and coinage under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>. Lower Navarre, north of the mountains, remained with the Albrets, whose heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry III</a> became King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xiii-of-france">Louis XIII</a> formally united it to the French crown in 1620. Spanish Navarre kept its separate status for more than three centuries, but after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-carlist-war">First Carlist War</a>, in which many Navarrese fought for the Carlist cause, the law of 16 August 1841, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ley-paccionada">Ley Paccionada</a>, turned the kingdom into a province with a special fiscal regime.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Navarre, the Basque kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pamplona">Pamplona</a>, gave the royal houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> their line through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III the Great</a>, carried the pilgrim road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#santiago">Santiago</a> and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV</a>, while its fueros survived conquest to shape the self-government of modern Navarre.</em></p>

